import TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Dispute

/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Reformation — the Reformation's case, 1521–1546

Luther did not argue alone. In the twenty-five years between his first quarrel
with Rome and the Council of Trent's decree on justification in January 1547,
the men around him wrote the doctrine down again and again, in Latin, German and
English, and Catholic theologians answered them in print. This chapter hears
thirteen of those voices, on both sides, in the order they wrote, so that no
reply comes before what it answers. Each is quoted from a printed edition that
anyone can read in a public scan.

**Melanchthon, 1521.** Philipp Melanchthon, Luther's colleague at Wittenberg,
published the first Protestant handbook of doctrine, the *Loci communes*, in
December 1521. "Nothing of our works, however good they seem or are, is
righteousness," he wrote, "but faith alone in the mercy and grace of God in
Jesus Christ is righteousness", and, a few pages on, "therefore faith alone
justifies" (*Justificat igitur sola fides*). He argued from Abraham, who
"believed the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness" (Genesis 15:6),
and from Romans 4:5, where faith is counted to the one "who does not work but
believes".

**Zwingli and Eck, 1525.** At Zurich, Huldrych Zwingli printed his *Commentary
on True and False Religion* in March 1525. Paul, he wrote, "says that Christ is
of no use to us if righteousness come from our works, Gal. 2:16; and that grace
through Christ is of no avail if salvation be due to works, Rom. 4:4 and 11:6".
The same year Johann Eck, Luther's opponent at Leipzig in 1519, published his
*Enchiridion*, a handbook of answers to the Lutherans that went through many
printings. Its chapter on faith and works opens with the thesis that "faith does
not suffice without works", and that works, by "the grace of God accepting
them", are "meritorious of eternal life". Against the Lutheran reading of "the
righteous shall live by faith" he says that the heretic who adds "alone" "tears
and falsifies the text, since it nowhere says that the righteous lives by faith
alone", and he sets James 2:24 beside it: "a man is justified by works, and not
by faith only".

**Tyndale, 1528.** In England the doctrine was a heresy to be listed, and
Tyndale wrote from exile. *The Parable of the Wicked Mammon*, printed at Marburg
in May 1528, opens: "That faith only before all works and without all merits,
but Christ's only, justifieth and setteth us at peace with God, is proved by
Paul in the first chapter to the Romans". He met James directly. "Faith
therefore justifieth in the heart, and before God; and the deeds justify
outwardly before the world, that is, testify only before men, what we are
inwardly before God."

**Augsburg, 1530–1531.** On June 25, 1530, the Lutheran princes presented their
confession to the Emperor at Augsburg. Its fourth article teaches that people
"cannot be justified before God by their own strength, merits, or works, but are
freely justified for Christ's sake, through faith", citing Romans 3 and 4, and
its sixth quotes a father, then thought to be Ambrose: "he who believes in
Christ is saved, freely receiving remission of sins, without works, by faith
alone." On August 3 the Catholic theologians' answer, the Confutation, was read
to the Diet. It calls the Lutherans' "ascription of justification to faith
alone" "diametrically opposite the truth of the Gospel", and from 1 Corinthians
13:2, "though I have all faith … and have not charity, I am nothing", it says
that "St. Paul certifies to the princes and the entire Church that faith alone
does not justify." Faith is approved, "not faith alone, which some incorrectly
teach, but faith which worketh by love" (Galatians 5:6). Melanchthon's *Apology*
answered in 1531: "The particle alone offends some, although even Paul says,
Rom. 3, 28", and "therefore by faith alone we are justified." Of James 2:24 he
wrote that "if the adversaries do not attach their own opinions concerning the
merits of works, the words of James have in them nothing that is of
disadvantage."

**Barnes, about 1531.** Robert Barnes, an Augustinian friar turned Lutheran, put
an article titled "Onely fayth iustifieth before God" into his *Supplication* to
Henry VIII. He argued from Paul at Antioch, "by hym are all that beleue,
iustified from all thynges" (Acts 13:39), and from the fathers: Ambrose on
Romans 3, "they doing nothing, nor nothing deseruing, alonely by fayth are
iustified by the gift of God".

**Cajetan, 1532, and Luther, 1535.** Cardinal Cajetan, who had examined Luther at
Augsburg in 1518, wrote at Rome in May 1532 that "the common teaching of the
church is most true, that the remission of sins comes not through unformed faith
but through faith formed by charity". That is the schoolmen's distinction: a
faith that only believes is "unformed", and it becomes the faith that justifies
when love (*caritas*) gives it its form and life (*fides caritate formata*). Of
Galatians 5:6, he said, Paul "clearly ascribes power in Christ not to faith of
any kind but to faith working through love." Luther, lecturing on Galatians in
1535, named that teaching as the corruption Paul fought. On 2:4–5, where Paul
would not yield "so that the truth of the gospel would remain", he said: "the
truth of the gospel is, that our righteousness cometh by faith alone, without
the works of the law. The corruption, or falsehood of the gospel is, that we are
justified by faith, but not without the works of the law. … Even so do our
Papists at this day. For they say, that we must believe in Christ, and that
faith is the foundation of our salvation: but it justifieth not, except it be
furnished with charity."

**Sadoleto and Calvin, 1539.** In March 1539 Cardinal Jacopo Sadoleto wrote to
the people of Geneva, whom the Reformers had won, to call them back. He used the
Reformers' own words in his own sense: "we obtain this blessing of complete and
perpetual salvation by faith alone in God and in Jesus Christ", but "faith is a
term of full and ample signification", which includes "the hope and desire of
obeying God, together with love", and "in this very faith love is essentially
comprehended as the chief and primary cause of our salvation." He cited Titus
2:14, a people "zealous of good works". John Calvin answered in September: "But
faith, you say, is a general term, and has a larger signification. I answer,
that Paul, whenever he attributes to it the power of justifying, at the same
time restricts it to a gratuitous promise of the divine favour." From Romans
4:6–8, the blessing of the one to whom God counts righteousness apart from
works, he concluded: "We deny that good works have any share in justification,
but we claim full authority for them in the lives of the righteous."

**Regensburg, 1541.** The last attempt at agreement came at the colloquy of
Regensburg in May 1541, where Melanchthon and Martin Bucer sat with Eck,
Johannes Gropper and the papal legate, Cardinal Gasparo Contarini. Their fifth
article, on justification, was accepted by the speakers of both sides. It says
that "the sinner is justified through a living and efficacious faith"; that "the
justifying faith is that faith which is efficacious through love; but meanwhile
this is true, that we are justified by this faith in so far as it lays hold of
the mercy and righteousness which is imputed to us for Christ's sake"; and that
the faithful soul "does not rest on" a righteousness inherent in it, "but on the
righteousness of Christ alone, given to us", as Paul counted all things loss
"not having a righteousness of my own" (Philippians 3:8–9).

Both sides then read the same words their own way. On May 11 Calvin, who was
there, wrote to Farel: "You will be astonished, I am sure, that our opponents
have yielded so much"; "nothing can be comprehended within it which is not to be
found in our writings." On May 25 Contarini explained the article as a "double
righteousness", one "inherent in us, by which we begin to be righteous", and one
"given to us with Christ", and read "efficacious through love" as "formed by
charity". In July the Protestant estates, in an answer Melanchthon wrote for
them, asked that the word "efficacious" be explained or struck out, because some
read it as "faith with works"; by "efficacious" they had meant that "faith itself
is a burning motion, consoling, obeying Christ, and giving life."

**An answer proposed here.** Paul writes that love "comes from a pure heart, a
good conscience, and sincere faith" (1 Timothy 1:5), and that "the fruit of the
Spirit is love" (Galatians 5:22). Whoever believes is "a slave belonging to
Christ" (1 Corinthians 7:22), obedient "from the heart" (Romans 6:17–18), and a
slave wants what his Master wants. So love is faith's fruit, and not its form.
And "there is no one who is righteous, not even one" (Romans 3:10), so no one has
a righteousness of his own beside Christ's. Both steps are the chapter's own,
marked proposed.

**What the debate decides.** Every step in it is rated `disputed`, because
each side grants the other's texts and denies its conclusion. Rome's reason is
that God renders to each according to his works (Romans 2:6) and that without
love all faith is nothing (1 Corinthians 13:2); the Reformers' reason is that
Paul counts righteousness apart from works (Romans 4:6), and that love follows
faith as its fruit and is not what makes it justify. So the Confutation
and Eck cite Romans 3 and Galatians, the *Apology* and Tyndale take up James 2,
Luther and Calvin read Galatians 5:6 and the texts on reward, and Calvin and
Contarini read the same article. So each attack across the line is met by one in
return, and only the Regensburg article, in its own words, is forced
(`before_trent_only_regensburg_is_forced`): no one before Trent denies what it
says. It can be held with the Reformers (`the_reformers_stand_together`) and
with Rome (`rome_and_regensburg_stand_together`). Where it stands is decided by
how its "efficacious through love" is read, and each reading falls at a step
rated `disputed` (`whatTheRegensburgFaithIs`). Put as a truth table for one
person, the texts from Paul, Hebrews and John make him justified exactly when he
believes, whatever his works (`justified_exactly_when_he_believes`). The sides
part at two claims: whether the faith that justifies does so alone or as formed
by love, and whether good works, made worthy by grace, are a ground of
salvation. Trent took up both in 1547.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaFide

open Testimony Testimony.Bib Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework Testimony.Scripture

/-! ### The texts both sides cited -/

/-- A text both sides of the Reformation accepted, cited to Scripture alone. -/
def reformationText (label : String) (refs : List ScriptureCitation) : AtomMeta :=
  { label := label
  , kind := .textual
  , source :=
      { primary := .scripture refs
      , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
      , confidence := .consensus } }

/-- The sources of the Reformation's debate: Scripture alone for each text the
voices cite, so that no later reader is heard on them, and for every other
claim the sources the argument gives it elsewhere. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def reformationCite : Claim → AtomMeta
  | .romans3_28 =>
    reformationText "Romans 3:28: a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.romans, 3, 28⟩ }]
  | .romans4_4_5 =>
    reformationText "Romans 4:4–5: to the one who does not work but believes, faith is counted"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.romans, 4, 4, 4, 5⟩ }]
  | .ephesians2_8_9 =>
    reformationText "Ephesians 2:8–9: saved by grace through faith, not of works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.ephesians, 2, 8, 2, 9⟩ }]
  | .galatians2_16 =>
    reformationText "Galatians 2:16: no one is justified by works of the law"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.galatians, 2, 16⟩ }]
  | .galatians1_6_9 =>
    reformationText "Galatians 1:6–9: a different gospel is no gospel, and is accursed"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.galatians, 1, 6, 1, 9⟩ }]
  | .gen15_6AbramBelieved =>
    reformationText "Genesis 15:6: Abram believed the LORD, and it was counted as righteousness"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.genesis, 15, 6⟩ }]
  | .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead =>
    reformationText "James 2:14–26: faith without works is dead"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.james, 2, 14, 2, 26⟩ }]
  | .james2JustifiedByWorks =>
    reformationText "James 2:21–25: Abraham and Rahab were justified by works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.james, 2, 21, 2, 25⟩ }]
  | .romans4_6_8 =>
    reformationText "Romans 4:6–8: blessed is the one counted righteous apart from works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.romans, 4, 6, 4, 8⟩ }]
  | .rewardTextsPromiseReward =>
    reformationText "1 Cor 15:58; Heb 6:10; 10:35; 2 Tim 4:8: God rewards the labor of believers"
      [ { ref := .verse ⟨.firstCorinthians, 15, 58⟩ }, { ref := .verse ⟨.hebrews, 6, 10⟩ }
      , { ref := .verse ⟨.hebrews, 10, 35⟩ }, { ref := .verse ⟨.secondTimothy, 4, 8⟩ } ]
  | c => baseCite c

/-! ### Melanchthon's *Loci*, 1521 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1521.** Melanchthon, in the *Loci communes*,
pp. 164 and 168 of Plitt and Kolde's edition. Rated `disputed`, because the
Confutation of 1530 grants Paul's texts, citing Romans 3 itself, and denies that
faith alone justifies. Its reason is that God "will render to every one
according to his works" (Romans 2:6), and that Paul's "though I have all faith
... and have not charity, I am nothing" (1 Corinthians 13:2) "certifies ... that
faith alone does not justify" (Jacobs, pp. 212–213). -/
def melanchthonLociStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work melanchthonLoci (.page 168)
  , supporting := [.work melanchthonLoci (.page 164)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith alone justifies, as Melanchthon read Paul in 1521.** Abram "believed
the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness" (Genesis 15:6), and Paul
says that to the one "who does not work but believes in Him who justifies the
ungodly, his faith is counted as righteousness" (Romans 4:5). So "nothing of our
works … is righteousness, but faith alone", and "therefore faith alone
justifies". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def melanchthonLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Melanchthon: faith alone justifies (Loci, 1521)"
  , grounds := [p .romans4_4_5, p .gen15_6AbramBelieved]
  , step := ⋀ [p .romans4_4_5, p .gen15_6AbramBelieved] ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some melanchthonLociStep }

/-- Melanchthon's *Loci* of 1521, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def melanchthonCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  melanchthonLine.asPackage reformationCite "justification is by faith alone"

/-! ### Zwingli's *Commentary*, 1525 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1525.** Zwingli, in the *Commentary on True and
False Religion*, p. 137 of Heller's English. Rated `disputed`, because the
Confutation grants the texts and holds that "it is entirely contrary to Holy
Scripture to deny that our works are meritorious" (Jacobs, p. 211). Its reason
is that Scripture promises a reward, "every man shall receive his own reward,
according to his own labor" (1 Corinthians 3:8), and that grace is not set aside
by it: "our works of themselves have no merit, but ... God's grace makes them
worthy of eternal life" (p. 211). The *Apology* answers from the same verse that
the reward is given to those already justified: works merit "not for the
remission of sins, for grace or justification (for these we obtain only by
faith), but for other rewards", and "those who merit this are justified before
they do the Law" (Triglot pp. 175, 221). Paul is writing of those who build on
the one foundation, "Jesus Christ" (1 Corinthians 3:11), and of a builder whose
work is burned up he says that "the builder will be saved, but only as through
fire" (3:15). So the reward follows salvation, and does not decide it. -/
def zwingliStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work zwingliTrueAndFalseReligion (.page 137)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Grace avails nothing if salvation is due to works, as Zwingli read Paul in
1525.** No one is justified by works of the law (Galatians 2:16); to the worker
wages are owed, not a gift (Romans 4:4); and if it is by grace, it is no longer
by works (Romans 11:6). Zwingli reads "works" the same way in both verses, as
the argument needs. So no work is a ground of salvation: believers "through
faith … are sure of salvation", their sins "not imputed to them as such because
of their faith". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def zwingliLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Zwingli: grace avails nothing if salvation is due to works (1525)"
  , grounds :=
      [p .galatians2_16, p .romans4_4_5, p .romans11_6, p .worksKeepsItsSenseInRomans4And11]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians2_16, p .romans4_4_5, p .romans11_6, p .worksKeepsItsSenseInRomans4And11] ➝
        p .salvationNotByWorks
  , delivers := p .salvationNotByWorks
  , inference := some zwingliStep }

/-- Zwingli's *Commentary* of 1525, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def zwingliCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  zwingliLine.asPackage reformationCite "salvation is not by works"

/-! ### Eck's *Enchiridion*, 1525 -/

/-- **Who draws the step against faith alone, in 1525.** Eck, in the
*Enchiridion*, fols. 45r–51r of the 1536 printing. Rated `disputed`, because
Melanchthon's *Apology* grants James 2 and Galatians 5:6 and denies that they
exclude faith alone. On Galatians it grants "faith which worketh by love", and
denies that "on account of this love we receive the remission of sins", because
by faith "we are accounted righteous for Christ's sake before we love ...,
although love necessarily follows" (Triglot p. 155). On James it grants that
James "is right in denying that we are justified by such a faith as is without
works", and says that he does not "describe the mode of justification, but only
of what nature the just are, after they have been already justified" (p. 189). -/
def eckStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fols. 50v–51r")
  , supporting := [.work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fols. 45r, 48v")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith does not suffice without works, as Eck read Scripture in 1525.**
Faith without works is dead (James 2:17), Abraham was justified by works, and "a
man is justified by works, and not by faith only" (2:21–24); what counts in
Christ is "faith working through love" (Galatians 5:6). And "the righteous shall
live by faith" nowhere says "by faith alone". So justification is not by faith
alone. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def eckLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Eck: faith does not suffice without works (Enchiridion, 1525)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .galatians5_6]
  , step := ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .galatians5_6] ➝
      notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some eckStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from reward to merit, in 1525 and 1530.** Eck, in his
first proposition, and the Confutation, answering the Augsburg Confession's
fourth article. Rated `disputed`, because the Reformed grant that God rewards
the good works of believers and deny that the works merit it (Westminster
Confession, XVI.6). Their reason is "the great disproportion that is between
them and the glory to come, and the infinite distance that is between us and
God", so that "when we have done all we can, we have done but our duty, and are
unprofitable servants" (XVI.5, after Luke 17:10). -/
def meritStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work romanConfutation (.page 211)
  , supporting := [.work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fol. 45r")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Works made worthy by grace are meritorious, as Rome answered in 1525 and
1530.** God promises to reward the labour of believers: "the crown of
righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me" (2 Timothy
4:8, with 1 Corinthians 15:58 and Hebrews 6:10 and 10:35). Eck put it in his
first proposition, that works are "meritorious of eternal life, by the grace of
God accepting them", and the Confutation answered the Augsburg Confession that
"it is entirely contrary to Holy Scripture to deny that our works are
meritorious", while all Catholics "confess that our works of themselves have no
merit, but that God's grace makes them worthy of eternal life". So good works,
made worthy by grace, are a ground of salvation. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def meritLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Rome: works made worthy by grace are meritorious (1525, 1530)"
  , grounds := [p .rewardTextsPromiseReward]
  , step := p .rewardTextsPromiseReward ➝ notP .salvationNotByWorks
  , delivers := notP .salvationNotByWorks
  , inference := some meritStep }

/-- Eck's *Enchiridion*, as a position: faith does not suffice without works,
and works made worthy by grace merit eternal life. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def eckCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Eck, Enchiridion (1525)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [eckLine, meritLine] [] []
  , conclusion := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is not by faith alone"
  , inferences := [eckStep, meritStep] }

/-! ### Tyndale's *Wicked Mammon*, 1528 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to faith only, in 1528.** Tyndale, in *The Parable of
the Wicked Mammon*, pp. 46–47 of Walter's edition. Rated `disputed`, as
Melanchthon's is, because Eck and the Confutation grant Paul and deny the
conclusion: James says "not by faith only" (2:24), and without love all faith
is nothing (1 Corinthians 13:2). -/
def tyndaleStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work tyndaleWickedMammon (.pages 46 47)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Who reads James that way, in 1528.** Tyndale, pp. 61 and 119–120. Rated
`disputed`, because Eck grants James 2 and reads it the other way, as excluding
faith alone: James speaks of justification itself when he says that "a man is
justified by works, and not by faith only" (2:24), and so faith does not
suffice without works. -/
def tyndaleJamesStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work tyndaleWickedMammon (.pages 119 120)
  , supporting := [.work tyndaleWickedMammon (.page 61)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith only justifies, as Tyndale read Paul in 1528.** "I am not ashamed of
the gospel", and in it "the just or righteous must live by faith" (Romans
1:16–17, as Tyndale quotes it). So "faith only before all works and without all
merits, but Christ's only, justifieth and setteth us at peace with God". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale: faith only justifieth (Wicked Mammon, 1528)"
  , grounds := [p .romans1_16_17]
  , step := p .romans1_16_17 ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some tyndaleStep }

/-- **Deeds justify before the world, as Tyndale read James in 1528.** James
says that faith without works is dead, and that Abraham was justified by works
when he offered Isaac (2:17, 2:21). Tyndale reads the deed as a witness: "His
deed justified him before the world; that is, it declared and uttered the faith
which both justified him before God". Rahab too "before God … was justified by
faith, which wrought that outward deed", for she had confessed Israel's God
before she saved the spies (Joshua 2). So James and Paul agree, because "faith
therefore justifieth in the heart, and before God; and the deeds justify
outwardly before the world." -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleJamesLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale: deeds justify outwardly before the world (Wicked Mammon, 1528)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks]
  , step := ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks] ➝
      p .james2_24Compatible
  , delivers := p .james2_24Compatible
  , inference := some tyndaleJamesStep }

/-- Tyndale's *Wicked Mammon*, as a position: faith only justifies before God,
and the works of James 2 justify before the world. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale, The Parable of the Wicked Mammon (1528)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [tyndaleLine, tyndaleJamesLine] [] []
  , conclusion := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is by faith alone"
  , inferences := [tyndaleStep, tyndaleJamesStep] }

/-! ### The Augsburg Confession, June 1530 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in June 1530.** The Augsburg Confession, Articles IV
and VI, Triglot pp. 44–47. Rated `disputed`, because the Confutation answers
these very articles, granting Romans 3 and denying faith alone. It calls faith
alone "diametrically opposite the truth of the Gospel, by which works are not
excluded", because God "will render to every one according to his works"
(Romans 2:6), and Christ says that not everyone who says "Lord, Lord" shall
enter, "but he that doeth the will of my Father" (Matthew 7:21; Jacobs,
p. 212). -/
def augsburgStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work augsburgConfession (.pages 44 45)
  , supporting := [.work augsburgConfession (.pages 46 47)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Freely justified through faith, as the Augsburg Confession taught in
1530.** A person is justified by faith apart from works of the law (Romans
3:28), and faith is counted as righteousness to the one who does not work
(4:4–5). So people "are freely justified for Christ's sake, through faith", and,
in the words the sixth article quotes, "without works, by faith alone". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def augsburgLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Augsburg Confession: freely justified through faith (1530)"
  , grounds := [p .romans3_28, p .romans4_4_5]
  , step := ⋀ [p .romans3_28, p .romans4_4_5] ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some augsburgStep }

/-- The Augsburg Confession, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def augsburgCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  augsburgLine.asPackage reformationCite "justification is by faith alone"

/-! ### The Confutation, August 1530 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to faith formed by love, in August 1530.** The
Confutation, answering the fifth article, p. 212 in Jacobs. Rated `disputed`,
